this graphic seismograph shows the exact  moment at 11 o'clock on the 11th of  November 1918 when the guns on the  Western Front fell silent after four and  a half years of fighting the Great War  was over hours earlier just after 5:00  a.m. that morning Germany had signed an  armistice in a railway carriage north of  Paris the Allies had won from the  trenches of the eep salient to London  Paris and across Europe celebrations  broke out it was a time of relief albeit  surely bittersweet communities villages  and families had been destroyed names of  key battles Passchendaele Jutland the  Somme would enter the national psyche  forevermore I don't think any of us  expected it to resonate quite how it has  resonated with the British population  and of course the reason it's resonated  is because of the personal connections  and the personal stories and people have  been able to identify with what their  great-great grandfather or their  great-great uncle  experienced and that's actually I think  being the case with the British soldiers  and indeed all our servicemen and women  as well who felt it like that the Queen  will lead the country in a special  moment of remembrance at the Cenotaph in  London  she'll be joined on Whitehall by the  German president in 1918 enemies in 2018  strong NATO allies as was the case last  year though Prince Charles will lay a  wreath on his mother's behalf the Duke  of Edinburgh is not expected to attend  veterans will march from Trafalgar  Square to the Senate are as they do  every Remembrance Sunday but this year  they will be joined by 10,000  descendants of soldiers who fought in  the Great War  lucy cubit will be one of them her  grandfather and two great uncles fought  only her grandfather survived it left an  indelible mark on the family so when  you're suddenly in within some two years  lose two of them I mean that's  shattering for any family isn't it the  centenary of World War one prompted Lucy  to put together books for her three  relatives letters regimental documents  photos an enduring record of their  service and sacrifice there's also  stories there's great bravery that's  have been handed on from generation to  generation all sorts of families around  the world who've been part of some of  these conflicts and have given their  children something to look up to the  centenary of World War one has focused  the continent on the events of a century  ago 16 million people on all sides lost  their lives a hundred years on that  hasn't been forgotten  Alastair Bunko Sky News
 

